# Deployment (CapRover)

> Portuguese: [pt-BR/deployment.md](pt-BR/deployment.md)

{{ project_name }} runs on [CapRover](https://caprover.com). One Docker image
serves two apps, chosen by `APP_ROLE`. Each workspace is served at
`<slug>.{{ base_domain }}` through one wildcard DNS record and one wildcard
certificate, so creating a workspace never touches DNS or CapRover.

| CapRover app | What | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `{{ app }}` | web: gunicorn on :8000 | `APP_ROLE=web`, runs the migrations |
| `{{ app }}-worker` | `manage.py db_worker` | `APP_ROLE=worker`, not exposed as a web app |
| `{{ app }}-db` | PostgreSQL (one-click app) | persistent data |
| `{{ app }}-redis` | Redis (one-click app) | shared cache |

**The code never calls the CapRover API, and the CapRover password never goes
into any app's environment.** Deploys use per-app tokens from your machine.

## Files

| File | Role |
|---|---|
| `Dockerfile` | `python:3.13-slim`, `uv sync --frozen --no-dev`, non-root user, `collectstatic` at build, `HEALTHCHECK` |
| `captain-definition` | tells CapRover to build the `Dockerfile` |
| `entrypoint.sh` | checks required env, waits for the DB, migrates, starts gunicorn or `db_worker` |
| `healthcheck.sh` | web: `GET /healthz/`; worker: PID 1 is `db_worker` |
| `deploy` + `scripts/caprover-deploy.sh` | `./deploy prod [web\|worker\|all]` |
| `.env.prod.example` | every runtime variable the code reads |
| `.env.deploy.example` | CapRover server, app names and App Tokens (local only) |

Commit `uv.lock`: the image installs exactly what it pins.

## 1. Create the apps

1. **PostgreSQL**: Apps > One-Click Apps > PostgreSQL, app name `{{ app }}-db`.
   Choose a strong password; set the database and user to `{{ project_slug }}`.
   Its data lives in a persistent volume created by the one-click app.
2. **Redis**: One-Click Apps > Redis, app name `{{ app }}-redis`, with a password.
3. **Web**: Apps > Create New App `{{ app }}`, tick *Has Persistent Data* only if
   you keep uploads on disk (then map the path `/app/media`).
4. **Worker**: create `{{ app }}-worker`. In *HTTP Settings* tick **Do not expose
   as web-app**. Keep one instance.

Inside CapRover, apps reach each other at `srv-captain--<app name>`.

## 2. Environment variables

Use `.env.prod.example` as the list. Set them in *App Configs > Environment
Variables* of each app, never in the repository or the image.

- **Both apps:** `SECRET_KEY`, `APP_FIELD_ENCRYPTION_KEY`,
  `APP_BASE_DOMAIN={{ base_domain }}`, `DB_*`
  (`DB_HOST=srv-captain--{{ app }}-db`), `REDIS_URL`, `MAIL_*`,
  `DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL`, `DJANGO_TASKS_BACKEND`, `ADMIN_URL_PREFIX`.
- **Web only:** `APP_ROLE=web`, `RUN_MIGRATIONS=1`, `GUNICORN_WORKERS`,
  `GUNICORN_THREADS` (2 or more), `GUNICORN_TIMEOUT`, `HEALTHCHECK_*`.
- **Worker only:** `APP_ROLE=worker`, `RUN_MIGRATIONS=0`,
  `DJANGO_TASKS_BACKEND=database`, `TASKS_WORKER_INTERVAL`, `TASKS_QUEUE_NAME`.

`entrypoint.sh` refuses to start when `SECRET_KEY`, `APP_BASE_DOMAIN` or a
`DB_*` credential is missing, and the worker refuses any task backend other
than `database`. Keep `DJANGO_TASKS_BACKEND` identical on web and worker:
`database` on the web app without a running worker leaves tasks queued forever.
Without Redis, use `GUNICORN_WORKERS=1` (the local-memory cache is per process).

## 3. Wildcard subdomains (once per project)

Workspaces live at `<slug>.{{ base_domain }}`. The edge is set up once:

1. **Cloudflare DNS**: add `A *` and `A @` pointing to the CapRover server IP,
   both **Proxied** (orange cloud). The CapRover root domain (here
   `apps.{{ base_domain }}`, dashboard at `captain.apps.{{ base_domain }}`) keeps its
   own `A *.apps` record, DNS only, so its Let's Encrypt certificates still work.
2. **Cloudflare SSL/TLS**: mode **Full (strict)**. Never downgrade it.
3. **Origin certificate**: SSL/TLS > Origin Server > Create Certificate for
   `*.{{ base_domain }}` and `{{ base_domain }}`. Prefer generating the private key on
   the server and pasting a CSR, so the key never leaves it. Store both files
   on the CapRover host, readable only by root:

   ```bash
   sudo mkdir -p /captain/data/nginx-shared/cf-origin
   # cert.pem (certificate) and key.pem (private key, chmod 600)
   ```

   CapRover's nginx container sees that folder as `/nginx-shared/cf-origin/`.
   The Origin CA is only trusted by Cloudflare, so both records must stay proxied.
4. **nginx block**: in the web app's *HTTP Settings > Edit Default Nginx
   Configurations*, append the block below **after the last `<% } %>`** of the
   template. CapRover renders the template once per domain of the app, so the
   guard keeps the wildcard block from repeating; replace `apps.{{ base_domain }}`
   with your CapRover root domain.

   ```nginx
   <% if (s.publicDomain === '{{ app }}.apps.{{ base_domain }}') { %>
   server {
       listen              443 ssl;
       http2               on;
       server_name         {{ base_domain }} *.{{ base_domain }};
       ssl_certificate     /nginx-shared/cf-origin/cert.pem;
       ssl_certificate_key /nginx-shared/cf-origin/key.pem;
       client_max_body_size 50m;
       resolver 127.0.0.11 valid=10s;
       set $upstream http://<%-s.localDomain%>:<%-s.containerHttpPort%>;

       location / {
           proxy_pass $upstream;
           proxy_http_version 1.1;
           proxy_set_header Host $host;
           proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
           proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
           proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
           proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
           proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
       }
   }
   <% } %>
   ```

   No `listen 80` is needed: in Full (strict) Cloudflare always talks to the
   origin on 443. Do not also add `{{ base_domain }}` as a CapRover custom
   domain of this app. If you change the main 443 block of the template, copy
   the change here. A broken template makes `nginx -t` fail and CapRover keeps
   the old config, so check the app logs after *Save & Restart*.

The SDK already does the rest: `ALLOWED_HOSTS` covers `.{{ base_domain }}`,
unknown hosts get a 404 (`SHOW_PUBLIC_IF_NO_TENANT_FOUND=False`) and
`/healthz/` answers before tenant resolution, for any host.

## 4. First deploy

```bash
cp .env.deploy.example .env.deploy   # fill in the App Tokens
./deploy prod all                    # checks, web (migrates), then worker
```

- Each App Token comes from the app's *Deployment* tab ("Enable App Token").
- `./deploy` runs `scripts/check.sh` (ruff + pytest) first; `SKIP_CHECKS=1`
  skips it. `./deploy prod web` and `./deploy prod worker` deploy one app.
- The web container runs `migrate_schemas --shared`, `bootstrap_workspace` and
  `migrate_schemas` on every start; all three are idempotent.

Check it:

```bash
curl -sS https://{{ initial_workspace | replace('_', '-') }}.{{ base_domain }}/healthz/   # {"status": "ok"}
```

## 5. Workspaces and superusers

Run commands inside the web container, from an SSH session on the host:

```bash
CID=$(docker ps -q -f name=srv-captain--{{ app }}. | head -1)
docker exec -it "$CID" python manage.py bootstrap_workspace --schema acme --name "Acme Ltda"
docker exec -it "$CID" python manage.py create_tenant_superuser --schema acme
```

Every workspace has its own users: create a superuser per workspace (and one
with `--schema public` for the platform admin at `https://{{ base_domain }}/admin/`).
The workspace admin is at `https://acme.{{ base_domain }}/admin/`.

## 6. Zero-downtime deploys

- `collectstatic` runs at build time, so a new container serves static files as
  soon as gunicorn starts.
- The `HEALTHCHECK` (start period 60s) keeps Swarm from routing traffic to a
  container before `/healthz/` answers. `GUNICORN_THREADS` has a floor of 2 so
  a slow request never blocks the probe.
- Set the web app's *Instance Count* to 2 or more (needs Redis) so one instance
  keeps serving while the other restarts. Keep the worker at 1.
- Migrations must stay backwards compatible with the code still running: add
  fields first, remove them in a later deploy.

## 7. Daily maintenance jobs

CapRover has no built-in scheduler. Run these once a day from the host's cron:
- `ai_purge_content` erases stored AI prompts and outputs older than each workspace's retention setting;
- `flushexpiredtokens`, run in every workspace, removes expired refresh tokens.

```cron
# /etc/cron.d/{{ project_slug }} on the CapRover host
15 3 * * * root docker exec $(docker ps -q -f name=srv-captain--{{ project_slug }}-worker | head -1) python manage.py ai_purge_content
30 3 * * * root docker exec $(docker ps -q -f name=srv-captain--{{ project_slug }}-worker | head -1) python manage.py all_tenants_command flushexpiredtokens
```
